# @cosyte/fhir

FHIR R4 for Node.js and TypeScript: a resource model that keeps every value exactly as written, JSON
and XML codecs, layered validation, and a safety readout for status and negation. A `decimal` keeps
its exact text and never passes through a JavaScript number.

## When to use it

- "Parse this FHIR resource", "read this Patient JSON", "load this FHIR XML", keeping `0.010`,
  `120.0` or a large `integer64` exactly as written.
- Validate a resource (structure, R4 constraints, your profiles) and return an `OperationOutcome`.
- Before summarizing or displaying a resource, check status, `refuted`, `entered-in-error`,
  `not-done`, `doNotPerform` and any `modifierExtension`.
- Read `Observation.value[x]` by its actual type and compare units on the UCUM `code`.
- Tell a transaction Bundle from a batch, resolve references, stream a Bulk Data NDJSON export.

Use another skill when:

- The input is a C-CDA or CDA R2 XML document: `cosyte-ccda`.
- You need code-system content, ValueSet `$expand`, ConceptMap `$translate`, UCUM equivalence or
  RxNorm: `cosyte-terminology`.
- You are converting HL7 v2 messages to FHIR: `cosyte-transform` (v2 parsing itself: `cosyte-hl7`).
- You need de-identification: `cosyte-deid`. Synthetic fixtures: `cosyte-synth`.

## Install

```bash
pnpm add @cosyte/fhir
```

`npm install @cosyte/fhir` and `yarn add @cosyte/fhir` also work. Node `>=22.0.0`. Dual ESM and
CommonJS builds with type declarations for both. Zero runtime dependencies, no Node built-in imported.
Everything ships from one entry point; there are no subpath imports.

## Minimal example

The document is the README's synthetic Observation; every value in it is fabricated.

```ts
import { assertSafeToSummarize, FhirSafetyError, isList, parseResource } from "@cosyte/fhir";
import { readObservationValue, resolvePath, validateResource, type FhirNode } from "@cosyte/fhir";

const document = `{
  "resourceType": "Observation",
  "id": "syn-0001",
  "status": "final",
  "code": { "coding": [{ "system": "http://loinc.org", "code": "8480-6" }] },
  "subject": { "reference": "Patient/syn-0001" },
  "effectiveDateTime": "2026-01-05",
  "valueQuantity": {
    "value": 120.0,
    "unit": "mmHg",
    "system": "http://unitsofmeasure.org",
    "code": "mm[Hg]"
  }
}`;

const { resource, issues } = parseResource(document);
console.log(issues.map((issue) => issue.code)); // the 120.0 was kept exactly, and says so

// Refuse rather than summarize something that cannot be summarized honestly.
try {
  assertSafeToSummarize(resource);
} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof FhirSafetyError) console.error("not safe to summarize at", err.locations);
  else throw err;
}

// Branch on the value[x] type before touching a magnitude, and compare on the UCUM code.
const reading = readObservationValue(resource);
if (reading?.type === "Quantity") {
  // The magnitude is clinical data: compare it, do not log it.
  console.log(reading.quantity?.value?.raw === "120.0", reading.quantity?.code);
}

// resolvePath returns a repeating last segment as one list node: flatten it yourself.
const codings = resolvePath(resource, "code.coding").flatMap((node): readonly FhirNode[] =>
  isList(node) ? node.items : [node],
);
console.log(codings.map((node) => node.kind));

const result = validateResource(resource);
console.log(result.valid, result.issues.filter((issue) => issue.severity === "error").length);
```

Expected output. Most lines are values the README asserts; the rest follow from the type
declarations. We have not run this exact snippet.

```text
[ 'DECIMAL_PRECISION_AT_RISK' ]
true mm[Hg]
[ 'complex' ]
true 0
```

## Key API

- `parseResource` / `serializeResource` (JSON), `parseResourceXml` / `serializeResourceXml` (XML);
  readers return `{ resource, issues }`. `nodesEquivalent` compares JSON-read and XML-read models.
- `FhirDecimal`, `FhirInteger64`, `decimal()`, `integer64()`: exact-text numbers.
- `validateResource(resource, options?)`: `{ valid, issues, toOperationOutcome() }`, with `profiles`
  from `loadStructureDefinition` or `defineProfile` and an optional `terminology` service.
- `readSafety(resource)` (status, `negations`, `safeToSummarize`) and `assertSafeToSummarize`
  (throws `FhirSafetyError` with `locations` only).
- `readObservationValue`, `readQuantity`, `readMedicationDoses`; `readBundle`, `buildBundleIndex`,
  `resolveReference`; `streamNdjson`, `parseNdjsonLine`.
- Model helpers `getProperty`, `getAllProperties`, `isList`, `resolvePath`; errors `FhirCodecError`,
  `FhirXmlError`, `FhirSerializeError`, `InvalidProfileError`.

## Limits and known defects

- **`resolvePath` does not flatten a repeating last segment.** It flattens repeating elements it
  walks through, but when the final segment repeats it returns the list node itself
  (`kind: "list"`, members on `.items`). This holds in 0.1.11, and its JSDoc says so. On a
  Patient with two names, `name` and `name.given` return list nodes while `name.family` returns two
  values. Flatten with `isList` as in the example. `pathExists` is unaffected.
- FHIR R4 (`4.0.1`) only. R5 and DSTU2 can be read but are not validated against their own
  definitions. No typed per-resource models; R4's own constraints run on eight modeled types.
- No bundled terminology or US Core content: pass your own `profiles` and `terminology` service.
- `type` and `profile` slicing discriminators are not checked (`PROFILE_SLICE_UNCHECKED`), and the
  slices' own constraints are then not checked either; a slice whose sliced element declares no
  `slicing` is reported `PROFILE_SLICE_NO_SLICING` (since 0.1.8) and not checked. FHIRPath outside
  the supported subset is `INVARIANT_UNCHECKED`. Never report any of these as a pass. `valid` ignores
  warnings, so read `issues` too.
- A parsed resource can keep the parsed text in memory while it lives: strings of 13 or more
  characters are slices of the input. Copy what you keep, for example with `structuredClone`, or let
  the parsed result go.
- No spec-clean JSON from a model read out of XML, no unit conversion, and a transaction Bundle is
  read, never executed.
- `readSafety` fields such as `status` and `retracted` describe the root resource only; branch on
  `negations`, which covers `contained` resources and Bundle entries.
- Upgrading from 0.0.10 changes verdicts: R4 constraints now run with no profile supplied.

## PHI handling

The README says to treat every document you hand this library as PHI.

- It works in memory only: no socket, no disk, no fetch, no terminology call unless you pass one in.
- Diagnostics carry no values: an issue is a code and a FHIRPath location, though a location can
  repeat an element name from the document. `FhirXmlError` and `FhirCodecError` carry a code and an
  offset or location, never input text; NDJSON failures report the line number, never its content.
- You own transport, storage, retention, access control, audit logging and every log line your code
  writes. Log issue codes and locations, never the resource or its values.
- Use synthetic resources in tests and examples. Never paste real patient data into prompts,
  fixtures or logs.

